*.swp
*.swo
*~
test-trigger.md

# IDE
# Anchored to repo root (leading /), same reason as /lib/ above: an unanchored
# ".vscode/" also swallowed vscode-extension/.vscode/, which holds the shared
# launch.json/tasks.json the README's "press F5" step depends on.
/.vscode/
.idea/
*.sublime-project
*.sublime-workspace
.vs/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Docker volumes (local mounts)
pdf-output/

# Compiled files
*.pyc
*.pyo

# Speckit Auto-AI: local-only task-progress signal files, never meant to be committed
.speckit-auto-ai/

# GitHub Speckit tooling scaffolding: command/agent definitions speckit init
# generates (speckit.*.agent.md, speckit.*.prompt.md), plus
# copilot-instructions.md - in a Speckit + Speckit Auto-AI project this ends
# up entirely tool-generated too (Speckit's own <!-- SPECKIT ... --> block
# plus this extension's auto-injected progress-tracking block), both
# reproducible locally by their respective tools rather than needing to be
# committed. If you ever hand-write real custom instructions into
# copilot-instructions.md and want that specific version shared via git,
# force-add it past this rule: `git add -f .github/copilot-instructions.md`
# - once tracked that way it behaves like a normal file from then on.
.github/agents/
.github/prompts/
.github/copilot-instructions.md

# .specify/ scaffolding (templates, scripts, integration manifests/state,
# workflows, memory/constitution.md) - regenerated by `speckit init`/the
# Speckit CLI / speckit.constitution.
.specify/

# Kiro tooling scaffolding (specs, settings, task summaries)
.kiro/
.kiro-task-*.md

# graphify knowledge graph output - 22MB of generated artifacts, rebuilt
# locally with `graphify update .`.
graphify-out/

# Local AI-assistant context. These describe how one contributor's assistant
# should work in this checkout, not how the project works - README.md and
# DEVELOPMENT.md are the shared documentation. Keep your own copies locally;
# they are deliberately not shared through git.
#
# CLAUDE.md is the exception on this branch: it holds the architecture the
# standalone VSIX is being built against, including decisions (Typst, mermaid
# in the webview, bundled interpreter) that are expensive to rediscover.
.claude/
skills-lock.json
CONTEXT.md
